y was  diagnosed with  in addition to identifying the specific  type of ADHD a person exhibits  criteria put forth by the DSM-5 also  require professionals who diagnose ADHD  to include the severity of the disorder  as either mild moderate or severe  someone with mild ADHD would only  exhibit a few of the symptoms specified  in the DSM and symptoms would only  result in minor impairments in the  individual's social school or work  settings  at the severe level the individual with  ADHD would exhibit many symptoms beyond  the number needed to make the diagnosis  with several beings severe or associated  with marked impairments in Social school  or work settings  someone with moderate ADHD would have  symptoms or functional impairment that  fall between the mild and the severe  levels  there is no single test to diagnose ADHD  rather a comprehensive evaluation is  necessary to establish a diagnosis rule  out other causes and determine the  presence or absence of coexisting  conditions  such an evaluation requires time and  effort and should include a careful  history and a clinical assessment of the  individual's academic social and  emotional functioning as well as their  developmental level  to receive a diagnosis of ADHD a  person's symptoms must be present before  the age of 12.  adults who exhibit symptoms associated  with ADHD but never received an official  diagnosis as children can still be  diagnosed though if they are able to  demonstrate that they first experience  symptoms before they were 12.  several types of professionals can  diagnose ADHD including clinical  psychologists clinical social workers  nurse practitioners neurologists  psychiatrists and pediatricians  regardless of who does the evaluation  the use of DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for  ADHD is necessary to obtain an accurate  diagnosis  keep in mind though that sometimes  individuals can have more than one  diagnosis which complicates matters so  for example a child might be diagnosed  with ADHD and depression  or ADHD and a learning disability  other times individuals who have ADHD  are misdiagnosed because symptoms of  ADHD can overlap with those of other  disorders  some of the most common misdiagnoses  include conduct disorders anxiety  depression Tourette's Syndrome substance  abuse and autism spectrum disorders  this is one reason why it is important  to not diagnose someone at first glance  but to examine them over time and at  different time periods to see how their  symptoms develop and change  rushing to obtain a diagnosis is not  beneficial to anyone and can in fact  make the situation worse  although nobody really knows what causes  ADHD  researchers have been able to identify  some potential risk factors associated  with its development  most likely ADHD results from a  combination of factors that interact  with and influence one another  trying to separate out these factors to  determine how they contribute to the  onset of a disorder is a nearly  impossible task  at best we can only speculate as to what  factors may lead to the onset of a  disorder  even then though we don't know whether  it is the factors themselves that  contribute to the cause of the disorder  or whether there are secondary hidden  factors that we are failing to consider  here's an example  did you know that there is a positive  relationship or correlation between the  amount of ice cream consumed and the  number of violent crimes that are  committed  Yep they're related as one increases the  other increases  does one really influence the other  though  or is there some secondary hidden factor  that we are failing to take into account  here  what about the heat of Summer  when it's hot out  people tend to eat more ice cream  and believe it or not the number of  violent crimes increases during the hot  summer months  you see what I mean  we need to be very careful when we start  talking about factors and whether or not  they contribute to the causes of a  disorder like ADH