when it comes to executing that  plan  someone with ADHD would have great  difficulty doing this  neuropsychological deficit is  inattention  in particular difficulty with sustained  attention  if I asked you to sit and work on a  puzzle for 30 minutes straight without  stopping  could you do it  if so you are able to maintain sustained  attention on a task  if however you keep needing to get up  and walk around you fidget in your seat  you Daydream and you can't seem to stay  on task then you might have problems  with sustained attention  this type of deficit also relates to our  ability to function efficiently if you  can't stay on task it will take you  longer to get things done  what about hyperactivity  this is when someone is excessively  restless and overactive someone who  fidgets quite a bit  this obviously can lead to issues trying  to complete tasks in a timely manner  a fourth deficit involves problems  modulating gratification  what in the world does that mean  it means that individuals with ADHD are  driven toward immediate frequent  predictable and meaningful forms of  gratification  at the same time they have great  difficulty working toward a long-term  goal  if for example a child with ADHD really  wants to buy a toy that costs twenty  dollars  and I give them ten dollars with a  promise that I will give them another  ten dollars in a week so that they can  buy the 20 toy  they would more than likely spend the  ten dollars right away to get the  immediate reward rather than wait a week  for me to give them another ten dollars  this holds true even in older children  with ADHD who have learned the value of  money and the benefits that come from  saving it  One Way researchers have examined  delayed gratification in children is  through the famous marshmallow  experiment first conducted at Stanford  in the 1970s  here children are offered a choice of  receiving one marshmallow immediately or  two marshmallows if they wait 15 minutes  during the wait time the single  marshmallow is placed in front of the  child on a table and the researcher  leaves the room  children who can delay their  gratification to receive the two  marshmallows have been found to distract  themselves by refusing to look at the  marshmallow singing laying their head  down on the table Etc  children with ADHD have a lot of  difficulty averting their attention away  from the treat and thus usually fail the  test  the final deficit relates to emotional  regulation or lack thereof  individuals with ADHD appear to be on a  roller coaster ride of emotions when  they are happy they are so happy that it  disrupts the people around them  when they are unhappy they are so  unhappy that people are equally  disrupted they operate at the extremes  often going from one to the other  if you think about it these  neuropsychological abilities affect  multiple aspects of Our Lives whether it  be family social academic or vocational  when these abilities are intact we  perform in a consistent predictable  independent and efficient manner  failure to use and develop these  abilities however leads to unpredictable  behavior and inconsistency which affects  every aspect of Our Lives  researchers have shown through a variety  of studies that multiple areas and  Pathways in the brain are affected by  ADHD  some of the major brain areas involved  include the frontal lobes basal ganglia  system and the cerebellum  reductions in cortical volume have been  identified in these regions  and these brain areas also form brain  circuits so connections of neurons from  different brain regions that subserve  particular brain functions  four of these circuits have been  implicated in the neuropathology  underlying ADHD  the first is called the fronto striate  thalamic circuit  this circuit has bi-directional  interconnections between the prefrontal  and parietal cortices the basal ganglia  system and various nuclei in the  thalamus  it's believed that this circuit mediates  abilities such as working memory  response inhibition and sustained  attention  the second circuit called the cortico  cerebellar circuit focuses on the  cerebellum and its projections to the  prefrontal cortex singular gyrus and  some parietal regions this circuit is  involved with analyzing information  about the temporal structure of the  environment  damage to the Circuit would result in  errors related to time estimation  such as how long we think it is going to  take us to do something and perception  the third circuit involves connections  between the ventral portion of the  striatum and the basal ganglia system  and the orbital frontal cortex in the  frontal lobes  it's believed that damage to this  circuit is associated with an inversion  to delayed reinforcement  in other words people who have damage to  this circuit will accept an immediate  reward but not a delayed one  think back to the example where the  child spent a small amount of money  right away rather than saving it to buy  something more expensive  the last circuit involves connections  between the amygdala the prefrontal  cortex and the orbital frontal cortex  this circuit is believed to be involved  in the regulation of our emotions  damage to this circuit could result in  the roller coaster ride of emotions  often noted in individuals with ADHD  there are four basic categories of  treatment for people with ADHD  medication  therapy  Education and Training and school-based  programs  keep in mind that many individuals  benefit benefit from involvement in all  four of these categories at the same  time  let's start by looking at medication  the first line of treatment medication  wise for individuals with ADHD is  stimulants  you might be thinking why would anyone  give a stimulant to someone who is  hyperactive  believe it or not they are pretty  effective in reducing hyperactivity and  impulsivity and improving one's ability  to focus work and learn  but how  stimulants tend to increase the  neurotransmitter dopamine which  contributes to the ability of our  frontal lobes to function properly  commonly prescribed stimulants include  Ritalin Concerta vivance and Adderall  although there are others  if a person with ADHD has difficulty  taking stimulants maybe they cause  bothersome side effects  non-stimulants may be a helpful  alternative  in general these medications take longer  to start working than stimulants but  have been shown to improve focus  attention and impulsivity in a person  with ADHD either by boosting the  neurotransmitter norepinephrine as the  drug addamoxetine does or by stimulating  activity in adrenergic receptors as is  the case with the drug guanfacine  keep in mind that there are many  different types and brands of  medications each with their own  potential side effects and benefits  sometimes different medications  different dosages or even different  combinations of medications are needed  to find out what works best for each  individual  obviously a person would need to be  closely monitored by their prescribing  doctor to determine this  a second category of potential treatment  is behavioral therapy  research suggests that behavioral  therapy works best by helping patients  and their families learn how to better  cope with daily challenges and struggles  rather than directly changing ADHD  symptoms per se  for example for children and teens the  therapist might work with parents and  teachers to help the individual keep a  routine organize items and reward them  when they are successful at doing these  things  for adults therapists might provide them  with tools to help organize their life  keep routines and break large tasks into  smaller more manageable ones  this idea of teaching families and  teachers how to help children with ADHD  is also relevant to the next category  Education and Training  research shows that in order to reach  their full potential and succeed  children and adults with ADHD need  guidance and support from their families  and their teachers  Education and Training includes one  teaching parents the skills they need to  encourage and reward positive behaviors  in their children  two sharing stress management techniques  with parents so they can deal better  with their own frustrations and by  extension with their child's Behavior  and three having parents and families  join support groups so they can connect  with others who are dealing with similar  situations  the last category is school-based  programs  some schools offer special education  services to children with aedhd who  qualify  here education Specialists can help make  changes to classroom and homework  assignments in order to help the child  succeed  In Sum medication as well as a variety  of Support Services to help not only the  person with ADHD but also their families  are known to benefit individuals with  ADHD and maximize their best chances for  success  we've covered quite a bit in this  lecture  and I hope you now have a better  understanding of ADHD  join us in our next lecture as we  examine another disorder that is  frequently diagnosed in childhood  developmental dyslexia  bye for now
 

